1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,000
POV: you're 25,

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:04,060
you live in Europe, and every month,

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,180
you quietly put €200

4
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,320
into an S&P 500 ETF.

5
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:10,890
Thirty years later,

6
00:00:10,910 --> 00:00:12,100
the account says almost

7
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:13,770
a quarter of a million euros.

8
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:17,470
But this isn't a story about getting rich.

9
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:19,910
It's a story about what happens in between.

10
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:21,870
The doubts.

11
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:22,650
The crashes.

12
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:24,500
The friends who seem to do better.

13
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:26,790
And the boring months nobody talks about.

14
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:30,630
Every number in this story is a hypothesis:

15
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:33,020
7% a year, after fees.

16
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,750
Real life will be different.

17
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:36,800
This is not financial advice.

18
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:39,730
Let's go back to the beginning.

19
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:42,160
Age 25.

20
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:43,330
The first paycheck.

21
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:46,780
You just got your first real salary.

22
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,790
After rent, food and transport,

23
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:49,550
there's a little left at

24
00:00:49,570 --> 00:00:50,700
the end of the month.

25
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:53,700
Your friends know exactly

26
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:54,450
what to do with it.

27
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:55,450
New phone.

28
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:56,430
Weekend trips.

29
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:57,470
A car on credit.

30
00:00:58,830 --> 00:00:59,920
You don't judge them.

31
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:01,700
You just want something different:

32
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:03,730
to stop worrying about money one day.

33
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:06,040
Your bank says:

34
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:07,920
leave it in the savings account.

35
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:09,050
It pays almost nothing,

36
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:10,380
but at least it's safe.

37
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:13,390
But prices keep rising.

38
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:16,320
So money that sits still slowly loses value,

39
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:17,960
even if the number never moves.

40
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,200
So one evening, you make a decision.

41
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:22,250
Not a big one.

42
00:01:22,270 --> 00:01:23,950
Just €200 a month.

43
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:26,520
Before that, you put

44
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,150
three months of expenses aside.

45
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:29,670
An emergency fund.

46
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:30,890
Because life happens.

47
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:33,280
The first buy.

48
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:36,120
You open your broker app,

49
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:37,280
you type VOO, and...

50
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,530
nothing. Not available in your region.

51
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:42,660
That's the fund every American

52
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:43,890
video tells you to buy.

53
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:45,830
But Europe has its own rules,

54
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:47,460
and VOO doesn't follow them.

55
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,930
So you search for the European version:

56
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:54,940
an S&P 500 UCITS ETF, based in Ireland.

57
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:56,800
Same 500 companies.

58
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:01,210
The yearly fee is 0.07%.

59
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:02,640
On your first €200,

60
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:04,300
that's 14 cents a year.

61
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:07,560
The app shows €200.00.

62
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:10,580
The next day, €199.40.

63
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,000
Welcome to investing.

64
00:02:13,330 --> 00:02:14,760
For comparison, many bank

65
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:16,830
funds charge more than 1%.

66
00:02:16,850 --> 00:02:18,950
Remember that number, it'll come back.

67
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,050
You set up an automatic plan:

68
00:02:22,070 --> 00:02:25,230
every month, on payday, €200 goes in.

69
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:26,960
You don't have to think about it.

70
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:30,360
You now own a tiny piece of Apple,

71
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:33,850
Microsoft, Nvidia, and almost 500 other companies.

72
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:35,200
It feels like nothing.

73
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:37,150
Age 26.

74
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:38,620
The boring years.

75
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:40,900
The first months,

76
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:42,380
you check the app every day.

77
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:43,150
Up a little.

78
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:43,940
Down a little.

79
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:44,460
Up again.

80
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,320
One day the account is down €30.

81
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:50,520
You lose sleep over thirty euros.

82
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:52,240
It's almost funny, looking back.

83
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,420
After a while, you stop checking.

84
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,970
The plan runs on its own.

85
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,320
Your friend Max tells you about a coin

86
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:01,380
that tripled in a week.

87
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:02,950
He made more in seven days

88
00:03:02,970 --> 00:03:03,960
than you in a year.

89
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,130
You feel stupid.

90
00:03:06,150 --> 00:03:07,570
Slow. Old-fashioned.

91
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:12,230
Three months later, Max's coin is down 80%.

92
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:13,940
He doesn't talk about it anymore.

93
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,240
You didn't win big.

94
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:17,790
But you didn't lose big either.

95
00:03:17,810 --> 00:03:19,190
That's the deal you signed.

96
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:23,240
Some months you forget the plan even exists.

97
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:24,680
That's when it works best.

98
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:27,090
Age 28.

99
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:28,060
Love and debt.

100
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:30,720
At 28, you move

101
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:31,940
in with someone you love.

102
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:33,470
One evening, they show you

103
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:34,750
a number they've been hiding:

104
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:37,190
€4,000 of credit card debt.

105
00:03:38,530 --> 00:03:41,160
The interest is around 20% a year.

106
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,600
That's money leaking out every single month.

107
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:46,210
Here's a simple rule:

108
00:03:46,230 --> 00:03:47,970
no investment reliably beats

109
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:49,810
paying off debt like that.

110
00:03:49,830 --> 00:03:52,950
Paying it off is like earning 20%, guaranteed.

111
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:56,700
So you keep your €200 going,

112
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:57,630
and together you throw

113
00:03:57,650 --> 00:03:59,110
everything else at the debt.

114
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:02,340
Eight months later, it's gone.

115
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:03,520
You celebrate with pizza

116
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,760
on the floor of a half-empty apartment.

117
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:07,620
Age 30.

118
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:08,880
The first crash.

119
00:04:10,230 --> 00:04:13,290
Five years in, you've put in €12,000.

120
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:16,560
Your account shows about €14,300.

121
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:19,250
Then the news changes.

122
00:04:19,270 --> 00:04:20,170
A virus.

123
00:04:20,190 --> 00:04:20,940
Cities closing.

124
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:22,730
Markets falling every day.

125
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:25,560
It's like 2020:

126
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:26,580
in just a few weeks,

127
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,310
the S&P 500 lost

128
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:29,880
about a third of its value.

129
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:37,480
Your €14,300 becomes €9,450.

130
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:37,960
On paper, you just

131
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:39,910
lost almost five thousand euros.

132
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:43,110
Your finger is on the sell button.

133
00:04:44,470 --> 00:04:46,340
But you remember why you started.

134
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:48,160
You didn't need this money for years.

135
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:49,130
So you do nothing.

136
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:52,980
And your €200 keeps buying every month,

137
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,310
at lower prices.

138
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:56,050
Same money, more shares.

139
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:00,470
In 2020, about five months after the bottom,

140
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:02,480
the market was back at its old high.

141
00:05:03,830 --> 00:05:06,590
Nobody can promise it'll always be that fast.

142
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:08,440
Sometimes it takes years.

143
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:09,240
But the people who

144
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:10,850
sold locked in their loss.

145
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:12,890
Age 32.

146
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:13,940
The raise.

147
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:16,210
You get a raise.

148
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:17,840
Your colleague Tom gets one too.

149
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:19,100
He buys a new car.

150
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:19,560
You buy...

151
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:20,280
nothing new.

152
00:05:21,630 --> 00:05:22,880
Tom's car loses value

153
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:24,720
the day he drives it away.

154
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:27,790
Your €200 doesn't come with a monthly payment.

155
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:30,240
Not because you're cheap.

156
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,170
Because you already like your life.

157
00:05:32,190 --> 00:05:34,410
And extra money gives you something better:

158
00:05:34,430 --> 00:05:35,070
options.

159
00:05:36,430 --> 00:05:37,390
This is the most

160
00:05:37,410 --> 00:05:39,590
underrated move in personal finance:

161
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:42,360
when you earn more, don't automatically spend more.

162
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:44,590
Age 34.

163
00:05:44,610 --> 00:05:45,690
The house question.

164
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:48,960
At 34, everyone around you

165
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:50,370
is buying an apartment.

166
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:52,450
You wonder if you should stop investing

167
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:53,690
to save for a deposit.

168
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:56,670
Here's how you think about it.

169
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:57,660
Money you need in two

170
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:58,930
or three years shouldn't

171
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:00,120
be in the stock market.

172
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:02,780
Because the market can drop

173
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,410
by a third right when you need it.

174
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:08,030
So you split your money into two buckets.

175
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:10,960
The €200 keeps going for the long run,

176
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:11,980
and a separate savings

177
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,520
account grows for the house.

178
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:16,700
Different goals, different places.

179
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,250
That one idea saves people

180
00:06:18,270 --> 00:06:19,400
from a lot of panic.

181
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:21,750
Age 35.

182
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:23,030
The quiet milestone.

183
00:06:24,370 --> 00:06:24,900
Ten years.

184
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:26,520
120 squares.

185
00:06:27,870 --> 00:06:30,140
You've put in €24,000.

186
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:33,240
Your account shows almost €35,000.

187
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,280
And something quiet happens:

188
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:37,610
this year, your money earns

189
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:39,660
about as much as you put in yourself.

190
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,340
From here, you're no longer

191
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:43,610
doing all the work alone.

192
00:06:44,970 --> 00:06:46,620
Nobody throws you a party.

193
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:48,330
There's no notification.

194
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:50,720
Just a number on a screen, growing quietly.

195
00:06:52,070 --> 00:06:53,140
Age 37.

196
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:54,540
The fee nobody sees.

197
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:56,930
At a family dinner,

198
00:06:56,950 --> 00:06:58,810
your cousin says she invests too.

199
00:06:58,830 --> 00:07:00,470
Her bank picked a fund for her.

200
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:03,000
You ask about the fee.

201
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:03,980
She doesn't know.

202
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,210
You look it up together:

203
00:07:05,230 --> 00:07:06,890
1.8% a year.

204
00:07:08,230 --> 00:07:09,350
It sounds small.

205
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:12,590
1.8 versus 0.07.

206
00:07:12,610 --> 00:07:13,240
But over thirty years,

207
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:16,220
on €200 a month, here's the gap.

208
00:07:17,570 --> 00:07:21,050
You end up with about €244,000.

209
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:24,760
She ends up with about €175,000,

210
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:25,910
in the same market.

211
00:07:27,270 --> 00:07:29,140
Almost seventy thousand euros,

212
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:29,920
gone in fees she

213
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:31,070
never saw on a bill.

214
00:07:32,430 --> 00:07:33,910
She switches the next week.

215
00:07:33,930 --> 00:07:36,350
It's never too late, but every year counts.

216
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:38,460
Age 40.

217
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:39,890
Taxes and choices.

218
00:07:41,230 --> 00:07:42,480
At 40, you finally

219
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:43,550
look at the boring part:

220
00:07:43,570 --> 00:07:47,210
taxes. Every European country handles this differently.

221
00:07:48,570 --> 00:07:50,690
Some countries have special accounts

222
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:51,060
that cut the tax,

223
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:52,860
if you follow their rules.

224
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:55,520
In France, for example, there's the PEA.

225
00:07:56,870 --> 00:07:58,560
Your fund is accumulating:

226
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:01,320
the dividends are reinvested automatically.

227
00:08:01,340 --> 00:08:03,920
In some countries, that can save tax.

228
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,670
In others, it changes nothing.

229
00:08:07,030 --> 00:08:08,260
So the right account

230
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:10,160
can matter as much as the right fund.

231
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:12,300
Check your tax office's website,

232
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:13,760
or ask a local adviser.

233
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:17,040
You also check your fund once a year:

234
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:18,140
the fee, the size,

235
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:20,000
and that it still does what it says.

236
00:08:20,020 --> 00:08:20,860
Ten minutes.

237
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,420
That's all the maintenance.

238
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:24,830
Age 42.

239
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:26,020
The guaranteed offer.

240
00:08:27,370 --> 00:08:29,860
At 42, an ad follows you everywhere.

241
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:31,930
A man in a rented sports car,

242
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:33,490
promising 20% a month.

243
00:08:33,510 --> 00:08:34,330
Guaranteed.

244
00:08:35,670 --> 00:08:36,630
You do the math.

245
00:08:36,650 --> 00:08:39,380
20% a month would turn €1,000

246
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:42,000
into almost €9,000 in a year.

247
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:43,200
If that were real,

248
00:08:43,220 --> 00:08:44,300
he wouldn't need your money.

249
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:46,870
The rule is simple.

250
00:08:46,890 --> 00:08:49,810
High returns and a guarantee never come together.

251
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:51,480
When you see both, it's a trap.

252
00:08:52,830 --> 00:08:55,970
Your boring ETF has never promised you anything.

253
00:08:55,990 --> 00:08:58,000
That's exactly why you trust it.

254
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:00,300
Age 44.

255
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:01,040
The layoff.

256
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,870
At 44, your company announces cuts.

257
00:09:04,890 --> 00:09:06,410
Your name is on the list.

258
00:09:07,770 --> 00:09:08,490
It's scary.

259
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:09,660
But this is exactly

260
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:11,660
what the emergency fund was for.

261
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:13,590
Three months of expenses,

262
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:15,370
waiting since you were 25.

263
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:17,990
You don't sell anything.

264
00:09:18,010 --> 00:09:19,950
The emergency fund pays the bills,

265
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:22,090
and the €200 keeps going.

266
00:09:23,430 --> 00:09:25,680
Four months later, you find a new job.

267
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:27,910
Your investments never even noticed.

268
00:09:29,270 --> 00:09:31,890
That's the real job of an emergency fund.

269
00:09:31,910 --> 00:09:34,240
It protects your investments from your life.

270
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:36,580
Age 45.

271
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:37,460
The snowball.

272
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:39,520
Twenty years.

273
00:09:39,540 --> 00:09:41,310
240 squares.

274
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,570
You've put in €48,000.

275
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:48,060
Your account shows about €104,000.

276
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:49,800
More than double what you put in.

277
00:09:51,170 --> 00:09:54,780
This year, your money earns about €7,300.

278
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:57,480
You only add €2,400.

279
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,280
It earns three times more than you.

280
00:10:00,630 --> 00:10:01,780
That's compounding.

281
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,560
Your gains make their own gains.

282
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:05,060
At first it's invisible.

283
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:06,230
Then it's all you see.

284
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:08,660
If you had started

285
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:10,620
at 35 instead of 25,

286
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:12,660
with the same €200,

287
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:19,000
you'd have about €104,000 at 55, not €244,000.

288
00:10:20,330 --> 00:10:22,820
That's why starting early matters so much.

289
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:25,390
The first euros you invest work the longest.

290
00:10:26,730 --> 00:10:27,850
Age 47.

291
00:10:27,870 --> 00:10:28,850
The record high.

292
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:32,880
At 47, the market hits a new record.

293
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:35,890
On TV, experts say it's too expensive.

294
00:10:35,910 --> 00:10:37,480
Wait for the crash, they say.

295
00:10:38,830 --> 00:10:40,660
The problem is that nobody knows

296
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,760
when the crash comes.

297
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:44,480
Waiting often costs more than the crash itself.

298
00:10:45,830 --> 00:10:47,040
Records are normal.

299
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:48,380
A market that grows over

300
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:50,040
time keeps setting new ones.

301
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:52,350
So you don't wait.

302
00:10:52,370 --> 00:10:53,200
You don't guess.

303
00:10:53,220 --> 00:10:54,820
The €200 goes in,

304
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:56,890
like every month for twenty-two years.

305
00:10:58,230 --> 00:10:58,910
Age 50.

306
00:10:58,930 --> 00:11:00,040
The second drop.

307
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:05,520
At 50, your account is around €162,000.

308
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:08,900
Then the market falls again, like in 2022:

309
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:10,730
about 20% in a year.

310
00:11:12,070 --> 00:11:15,100
This time, 20% isn't a few thousand euros.

311
00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:17,480
It's about €32,000.

312
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:19,440
More than you saved in ten years.

313
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:21,890
Your hands are calm.

314
00:11:21,910 --> 00:11:23,290
You've seen this before.

315
00:11:23,310 --> 00:11:24,510
You know the only real loss

316
00:11:24,530 --> 00:11:26,290
is the one you lock in by selling.

317
00:11:27,630 --> 00:11:28,990
About two years later,

318
00:11:29,010 --> 00:11:31,010
the market is back above its old high.

319
00:11:32,370 --> 00:11:34,090
But it makes you think about something:

320
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:37,480
the S&P 500 is only American companies.

321
00:11:38,830 --> 00:11:41,490
Some Europeans prefer a world ETF,

322
00:11:41,510 --> 00:11:44,250
with thousands of companies from many countries.

323
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:45,820
It's a valid choice,

324
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:47,910
explained in our ETF video.

325
00:11:49,270 --> 00:11:50,250
Age 55.

326
00:11:50,270 --> 00:11:51,000
The number.

327
00:11:52,370 --> 00:11:53,230
Thirty years.

328
00:11:53,250 --> 00:11:55,010
360 squares.

329
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:56,570
Every single one filled.

330
00:11:57,930 --> 00:12:00,370
You've put in €72,000.

331
00:12:00,390 --> 00:12:03,940
The account says about €244,000.

332
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:06,000
Is that rich?

333
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:06,730
Not really.

334
00:12:06,750 --> 00:12:07,560
But it's freedom.

335
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:10,600
A common rule of thumb says you

336
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:12,630
could take out about 4% a year.

337
00:12:12,650 --> 00:12:15,650
That's around €9,800 a year,

338
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:17,420
or €800 a month.

339
00:12:18,770 --> 00:12:19,840
Not a fortune.

340
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:21,350
But €800 a month,

341
00:12:21,370 --> 00:12:23,400
every month, without working for it.

342
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,710
That changes how you say yes and no.

343
00:12:27,070 --> 00:12:28,650
And remember inflation:

344
00:12:28,670 --> 00:12:30,050
in today's money,

345
00:12:30,070 --> 00:12:36,560
€244,000 in thirty years is worth about €135,000.

346
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:39,760
So no, you're not a millionaire.

347
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:41,360
You're something quieter:

348
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:42,410
someone who doesn't panic

349
00:12:42,430 --> 00:12:43,820
when the car breaks down.

350
00:12:45,170 --> 00:12:46,580
And the strangest part?

351
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:47,590
The hardest thing you

352
00:12:47,610 --> 00:12:48,980
did wasn't picking a fund.

353
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,160
It was staying still.

354
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:52,770
If you remember one thing:

355
00:12:52,790 --> 00:12:54,960
time, a cheap fund, and not stopping.

356
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:56,200
That's the whole trick.

357
00:12:57,530 --> 00:12:58,780
And if you're 35,

358
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:00,570
45 or 50 right now,

359
00:13:00,590 --> 00:13:01,650
it's not too late.

360
00:13:01,670 --> 00:13:03,870
The squares just start from where you are.

361
00:13:05,230 --> 00:13:06,710
All numbers here assume

362
00:13:06,730 --> 00:13:08,590
7% a year after fees.

363
00:13:08,610 --> 00:13:10,560
Markets can go down for years,

364
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:12,060
and nothing is guaranteed.

365
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:14,000
This is not financial advice.

366
00:13:15,370 --> 00:13:17,340
Maybe you're 25 right now.

367
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,060
Maybe this is your first month.

368
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:20,160
Square number one.

369
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:23,930
If this story felt familiar, subscribe.

370
00:13:23,950 --> 00:13:24,820
Next up:

371
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,950
every type of ETF, explained.

